WEBVTT

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:07.420
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:14.420
As we are going to model hardware in our labs this semester, we would need to know something about hardware

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:23.210
description languages in general. So here is the introduction to hardware description languages.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:32.210
If you think about traditional programming languages, most of them have been modeled after sequential processing.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:39.530
So basically, operations in the program are performed in sequence, one after another,

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:45.810
the way that you write the code is going to be the way that the instructions are executed.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:50.130
So that resembles the basic computer model.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:55.290
Nowadays there are more recent developments, there are lots of parallel programming models

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.490
where sequential code is being split into threads,

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.530
and those threads are run in parallel hardware.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.050
However, inside of those threads,

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.330
again, most of the time, sequential computer

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:16.330
and programming models are being used.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.090
Hardware description language, on the other side,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:26.090
is made to support digital hardware.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.970
So if you look at characteristics

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.290
of digital hardware components,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.290
is first of all that you do have components or parts

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.330
that the hardware is made out of.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.750
So all of these components are connected in a certain way.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.290
Interesting thing when you look at this

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.730
from the modeling perspective

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.430
is that all of those parts work concurrently.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.430
So basically, operations in a hardware description language

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.090
need to be concurrent as well.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.870
Moreover, in real life, each of those parts

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:09.870
or components or hardware does not execute the operation

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.330
in zero time, but it has propagation delay.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:18.930
and therefore the language needs to model propagation delay

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.690
as well as timing,

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:25.690
because now timing of when the operations are executed

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.170
needs to, will be dependent on the propagation delay

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.110
and the basically of the component itself.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.050
These characteristics of digital hardware

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.650
are very difficult to be captured

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.710
by traditional programming language.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.630
you need to do lots of extra programming.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.630
So therefore, new paradigm has appeared

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.430
that is like hardware description languages.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.410
So they are used for following three different things.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.490
First and foremost,

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.570
hardware description language can be used

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.890
as formal documentation to describe the system,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.430
its functionality, structure of the system

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.530
in terms of components and connections and timing.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:24.410
hardware-shaped language can be used to help with prototyping, and that is how we are going

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:30.610
to start using it. It's going to be an input to a simulator where we describe our system,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:36.210
and then we simulate it and figure out if it works up to specification and perform desired

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:44.490
function. If that step has been satisfied and functionality is being proven, the next

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.190
Next step would be to implement design.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:53.490
So Harvard description language also can be input to a synthesis tool.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:04:02.050
And that is our rivado that we are using this semester.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:07.450
And we will look at the part of rivado that implements a synthesis further on.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:15.530
So basically, we can follow from description and documentation through simulation all

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:23.450
the way to the synthesis, the design cycle using hardware description languages.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:32.250
When we talk about synthesis, language constructs and components modeled by language are basically

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:40.090
map to circuits or components in libraries and that's how the designs are being implemented.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:50.200
Today's modern card description languages do have certain characteristics. So they need to

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:59.400
capture parts. In our case they are going to be called modules. They need to capture how

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:07.720
are those parts connected, they need to model concurrency of execution on those parts,

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:16.040
and they need to update accurate timing. Two hardware description languages are supported

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:22.040
by most of the software tools and their industrial standards, their VHDL and Verilog.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:29.480
syntax and appearance of those languages are somewhat different but scopes and

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:35.480
capabilities are quite similar. Once you learn one of them it will take very

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:41.920
short amount of time to be able to code in the other one. In this

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:48.680
course we are going to focus on Verilog and further modules in this

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:56.920
tutorial will be describing one by one essential parts of Verilog.

